.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt FCNTL 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm fcntl ,
.Nm fcntl64
.Nd control a file descriptor
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In fcntl.h
.Ft int
.Fn fcntl "int fd" "int cmd" "..."
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn fcntl
performs
.Fa cmd
on the open descriptor
.Fa fd .
The optional third argument is an integer or a pointer, as specified
by the command.
Musl implements this function through i386 system call 221,
.Nm fcntl64 ;
it declares no separate
.Fn fcntl64
function.
The kernel takes the descriptor in EBX, the command in ECX, and the
third argument in EDX.
.Pp
The supported commands are:
.Bl -tag -width F_DUPFD_CLOEXEC
.It Dv F_DUPFD
Duplicate
.Fa fd
onto the lowest unused descriptor at or above the integer argument.
The new descriptor shares the open-file description and has
.Dv FD_CLOEXEC
clear.
Return the new descriptor.
.It Dv F_DUPFD_CLOEXEC
As
.Dv F_DUPFD ,
but set
.Dv FD_CLOEXEC
on the new descriptor.
.It Dv F_GETFD
Return
.Dv FD_CLOEXEC
if it is set, or 0 otherwise.
.It Dv F_SETFD
Set or clear
.Dv FD_CLOEXEC
according to that bit in the integer argument.
Ignore other bits.
.It Dv F_GETFL
Return the access mode,
.Dv O_RDONLY ,
.Dv O_WRONLY ,
or
.Dv O_RDWR ,
combined with the stored
.Dv O_APPEND
and
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
flags.
A descriptor opened with
.Dv O_PATH
reports
.Dv O_RDONLY
and
.Dv O_PATH .
.It Dv F_SETFL
Replace the open-file description's
.Dv O_APPEND
and
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
flags with those bits from the integer argument.
Ignore other bits, including the access mode and
.Dv O_CLOEXEC .
.It Dv F_GETLK , F_GETLK64
Write
.Dv F_UNLCK
to the first short integer,
.Va l_type ,
in the caller's lock structure.
Leave its other fields unchanged.
No conflicting lock is reported.
.It Dv F_SETLK , F_SETLKW , F_SETLK64 , F_SETLKW64
Return success without inspecting the lock structure, storing a lock,
or waiting.
These operations provide no exclusion between processes.
.El
.Pp
Every other command is refused with
.Er EINVAL .
The kernel accepts both the older lock command numbers 5, 6, and 7
and the 64-bit lock command numbers 12, 13, and 14.
A descriptor opened with
.Dv O_PATH
takes
.Dv F_DUPFD ,
.Dv F_DUPFD_CLOEXEC ,
.Dv F_GETFD ,
.Dv F_SETFD
and
.Dv F_GETFL
and returns
.Er EBADF
for every other command, as on Linux.
.Pp
.Dv FD_CLOEXEC
belongs to one descriptor and causes it to close during
.Xr execve 2 .
The file offset and status flags belong to the shared open-file
description, so changes are visible through descriptors duplicated by
.Xr dup 2
or inherited by
.Xr fork 2 .
.Dv O_APPEND
selects the file end for regular-file writes.
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
affects operations that implement nonblocking I/O, including socket
connection and receive operations.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The duplication commands return a new descriptor.
.Dv F_GETFD
and
.Dv F_GETFL
return the requested flags.
The other supported commands return 0.
On failure,
.Fn fcntl
returns -1 and sets
.Va errno ,
except that musl's
.Dv F_GETOWN
fallback returns the raw -22 of
.Er EINVAL
for an open descriptor without setting it.
The kernel interface returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/ofdprobe.c
sets
.Dv FD_CLOEXEC
with
.Dv F_SETFD ,
reads it back with
.Dv F_GETFD ,
then duplicates the descriptor with
.Xr dup2 2
and checks that the new descriptor's flag is clear.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EINVAL]"
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa fd
is not an open descriptor, or was opened with
.Dv O_PATH
and the command is not one it takes.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
A lock query cannot write
.Va l_type
through the supplied pointer.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The command is unsupported, or a duplication command's minimum
descriptor is negative or at or above the process's descriptor limit.
.It Bq Er EMFILE
No free descriptor exists at or above the requested minimum and below
the limit, or the required descriptor storage could not be allocated.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
There is no current process.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dup 2 ,
.Xr execve 2 ,
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr open 2 ,
.Xr prlimit64 2
